10472	{"artifactId":"LLD-8ab2cc2edb3743df-s1","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-8ab2cc2edb3743df-s1-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-10T10:36:18.011Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"pass","summary":"review: pass","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"pass","findings":[{"claimId":"fix-targets-defect.1","ref":"fix-targets-defect","item":"fix-targets-defect","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The root cause is that the plan walk stores one value per output name and a later producer replaces an earlier one; changing the walk to append and project lists removes it and covers all five points of the ISSUE's fix intent.","evidence":"src/analyze/executor/walker.ts:110 `const outputs = new Map<string, unknown>();`, :198-200 `for (const [name, value] of Object.entries(result.outputs)) { outputs.set(name, value); }`, and projectUpstream (:463-474) copies one value per consumed name. A planner task's report goes through the same loop (record.outputs = { [task.produces[0]!]: childResult.root.finalReport }, :343), so the nested-plan point is covered by the same change. The design's sections map to the ISSUE's fix intent: every output in plan order with its task (3.2, 2.3), prompt with task and params (2.2), failed producers reported absent (2.4), one-producer plans unchanged (2.2 postcondition), all families and nested reports (3.1 call sites, 3.2). The prompt-size limit is left out, as the ISSUE's own 'Not in scope' says. [files: src/analyze/executor/walker.ts, docs/standalone/bug-plan-tree-analyze-run-when-E202610098ab2cc2e/ISSUE.md]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Today absentInputsFor leaves out a failed or skipped producer when another task produced the same name, and aggregateUnmet / unmetDependencies test only whether a name is present.","evidence":"walker.ts:444-448 `// Another task may have produced the same name; it is then not absent.` / `if (outputs.has(name)) continue;`. aggregateUnmet :416 `if (consumes.some(name => outputs.has(name))) return null;`. unmetDependencies :390 `if (!outputs.has(name)) { return name; }`. [files: src/analyze/executor/walker.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"All five aggregate system prompts describe one `### <taskId>` section per input, while the code titles each block with the output name.","evidence":"code.aggregate.system.md:12, data.aggregate.system.md:14, infra.aggregate.system.md:14, generic.aggregate.system.md:20 and docs.aggregate.system.md:10 each read \"A block titled `Upstream task outputs:` with one `### <taskId>` section per prior task\". renderUpstreamSection (aggregator.ts:189-208) iterates `Array.from(map.keys()).sort()` and writes `### ${id}`, where the keys are the consumed output names handed over by projectUpstream. [files: src/prompts/analyze/code.aggregate.system.md, src/prompts/analyze/data.aggregate.system.md, src/prompts/analyze/docs.aggregate.system.md, src/prompts/analyze/generic.aggregate.system.md, src/prompts/analyze/infra.aggregate.system.md, src/analyze/runtimes/shared/aggregator.ts]","action":"None. Note for the build: the same sentence in each prompt also says the body is `[unavailable: ...]` \"when the task failed\"; with the new absent rule a failed task has no sub-section at all, so that clause is worth rewording in the same edit.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The only production reader of upstreamOutputs is the shared aggregate code, reached through the five aggregate-report runtimes; the walker is the only producer of the map.","evidence":"A repo-wide search for `upstreamOutputs` in .ts/.tsx/.kt finds non-test hits only in executor/walker.ts (:246, :251), executor/types.ts (:69), runtimes/shared/aggregator.ts (:60, :108, :136, :148, :162, :167) and the five runtimes/{code,data,docs,generic,infra}/aggregate-report.ts, each passing `upstreamOutputs: args.upstreamOutputs`. The adherence code no longer reads it (scope-sources.test.ts:98 and adherence-topic-route.test.ts:443 assert that), so the ISSUE's mention of the adherence code as a reader is out of date and the design is right to leave it out. The context shaper's `upstreamTasks` (context/driver.ts:653) is a different map fed from the RPC, not from the walk. [files: src/analyze/executor/walker.ts, src/analyze/executor/types.ts, src/analyze/runtimes/shared/aggregator.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The migration's list of test files to update is every test that passes a non-empty map or reads the map it was handed.","evidence":"Search results match the list: aggregator.test.ts (:142, :219, :249, :323, :331 and the _renderUpstreamSectionForTest calls at :110-126), aggregate-absent-inputs.test.ts:38, walker-aggregate.test.ts:76 and walker-walk-failure.test.ts:74 (both `Object.fromEntries(args.upstreamOutputs)`), walker.test.ts (uses _projectUpstreamForTest and _unmetDependenciesForTest at :127-146, which the search for the field name alone would miss, and the design lists it), completeness-all-runtimes.test.ts:73 (`new Map(Object.entries(upstream))`), and live suites for code, data, generic and infra only (there is no docs aggregate-report.live.test.ts). The remaining hits pass `new Map()`. The live suites assert `report.metadata.tasksAnalyzed === SYNTHETIC_UPSTREAM.size`, which stays true with one output per name. [files: src/analyze/executor/__tests__/walker.test.ts, src/analyze/runtimes/shared/__tests__/aggregator.test.ts, src/analyze/runtimes/__tests__/completeness-all-runtimes.test.ts]","action":"None. item-length.test.ts imports `_buildMessagesForTest` from summariser/driver.js, not from the aggregate code, so it needs no look at all.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"preserved-behaviour.1","ref":"preserved-behaviour","item":"preserved-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"For a plan with one producer per name, the report task's user message, the absent list and the counts (tasksAnalyzed, upstreamTasks) are what they are today.","evidence":"Today both counts are `args.upstreamOutputs.size` (aggregator.ts:136, :148), the number of names; a sum of one output per name is the same number (aggregator.test.ts:232 asserts 3 for three names). With one producer per name, a failed producer's name is never in `outputs`, so the removed `if (outputs.has(name)) continue;` (walker.ts:446) never fired and the absent list is the same; no absent name then has an output, so renderAbsentSection keeps today's wording (asserted verbatim at aggregator.test.ts:350-353 and aggregate-absent-inputs.test.ts:59-60). One detail the design words loosely: today's 'unavailable' line is written for `out === null || out === undefined` (aggregator.ts:197), and the design says 'a null value'. [files: src/analyze/runtimes/shared/aggregator.ts, src/analyze/executor/walker.ts]","action":"When building, keep the unavailable test as `value === null || value === undefined` for both the single block and a sub-section; a null-only test would render an undefined value as a json fence and break the byte-identity promise.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"preserved-behaviour.2","ref":"preserved-behaviour","item":"preserved-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"UpstreamOutput can be filled from what the walk already has for every producer, including a planner task, and INV-6 keeps two tasks of one template on the same names.","evidence":"PlannedTask (src/shared/analyze-types.ts:68-77) has `taskId`, `template` and `params: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>` for leaf and planner tasks alike, the same type the design gives UpstreamOutput.params. The accumulate step (walker.ts:195-201) runs after either dispatch with `task` in scope. validate.ts:290-306 (INV-6) compares the sorted `produces` of each task with the template's and is not touched by the design. [files: src/shared/analyze-types.ts, src/analyze/planner/validate.ts, src/analyze/executor/walker.ts]","action":"None.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each acceptance criterion has a test that would fail on today's code, and the existing harness can carry them.","evidence":"walker-aggregate.test.ts:60-94 already has the stand-ins the design names: a producer returning `${n} from ${taskId}`, a broken producer, and an aggregate that records `upstreamOutputs` and `absentInputs`. Run on today's walker, the eight-producer test (ac1) would see one value, the last (walker.ts:199); the four-planner test (ac5) one report; the failed-sibling test (ac3) an empty absent list (walker.ts:446). ac2's render test would fail because renderUpstreamSection writes one block per key (aggregator.ts:195-206), and its prompt test because all five prompts still say `### <taskId>`. ac4 is held by an expected string taken from the code before the change plus the kept assertions at walker-aggregate.test.ts:113-119, :136-140. The existing assertions on raw values (:111, :135, :187) do have to be rewritten to the list form, as the design says. [files: src/analyze/executor/__tests__/walker-aggregate.test.ts, src/analyze/runtimes/shared/__tests__/aggregator.test.ts]","action":"Two points for the build. The prompt test should assert the old sentence ('one `### <taskId>` section per prior task') is gone and the new forms are present, not that the string `<taskId>` is absent: the new `#### <name> from task <taskId> (<template>)` line contains it. And the ac4 expected string must be captured from the unchanged code and pasted in as a literal, not computed by the new renderer.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":0,"low":8,"unverified":0},"template":"design-issue","reviewedAt":"2026-10-10T10:36:18.011Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
